hi this is steve kaufman here again um i  did um a video a little while ago about  about speaking and and um  you know answering the question how do  you get from this sort of passive  input based learning to actually  speaking  and  you know i am a believer in in a lot of  input and many people and of course it's  easy to do it has a lot of practical  advantages you can read when you want  you can listen when you want you can  listen when you do other things and all  of that is helping the brain get used to  a new language  i've said that i'm not a great fan of  implicit instruction and so forth but  one thing is for sure when you are  listening and when you are reading  you don't notice everything that's going  on in the language  uh so you need  to start to notice  and uh i'm gonna kind of go through i'm  gonna again as always i don't write  these things out so they're kind of  rambling and  i talk a lot about my own experience  which might be different from the  experience of others  but  the way i see it is  when i start a language  it's very difficult to notice anything  you're sort of  deep in this  dark well  and everything is dark and unclear and  and the first thing that i can start to  notice is words  by listening a lot  and of course by having the text  available to read  and by having if it's a traditional  system there'll be a word list of words  and so i'll i will gradually start to  identify the words i will start to  notice the words  it may be that i'll notice that for  example  in russian instead of saying i have they  say to me is  and that'll sound strange and i don't  really quite understand  what  is happening from a grammatical point of  view but i know that they're saying  um  i have so i start to notice these basic  structures these basic components  and so for the first long while i'm just  noticing words and phrases helped along  by my reading  and if we're using link then helped  along by the words and phrases that i  have saved  and  i'm not very sophisticated at that point  in in how i  how i save different words and phrases i  don't i might try to tag a little bit by  you know parts of the body or something  but i tend not to do that because it's  all scattered i'm picking up little  pieces here and there  and and of course the first thing that  starts to happen is  the more you have already noticed and  the more you understand the more you're  able to notice other things  so things that completely passed you by  the first time the second time saying  listening to something first time you're  listening just for meaning and now the  next time you're starting to listen for  some of the words and phrases that you  have saved  so as you are more familiar with the  context you notice more things that  relate to the language in the same way  the same way the more of the language  you've already noticed the more  additional things you can notice i mean  we know for example people who have a  large vocabulary can easily pick up more  vocabulary just by inferring the meaning  we don't have to look everything up in  the dictionary if we have enough of a  base we start to get what the new words  mean  so so our gradually our vocabulary grows  in this way and and i believe that what  we do at link with with our our you know  linking creating links and reviewing  them and flash cards  as long as they come from content that  that helps you notice and then when we  highlight the words in yellow so that a  word you have already saved before and  you've forgotten  you see it again highlighted in yellow  it helps you notice  uh so now let's move on to grammar  certainly and i've always said one  should have a short grammar book  available  just to look through the first time you  look through it you get a very vague  sense of what happens in the language  you can't possibly remember the detailed  descriptions of the rules and their  exceptions because you don't have enough  knowledge in order to notice that you  don't have enough knowledge to  understand the explanations  at some point  you  like i'm finding now with russian i'm  starting to focus on cases i have a  vague sense of the cases i'm able to  infer the meaning because i know enough  words  and i'm always more motivated to learn  more words because that's what's holding  me back from understanding the  content that i'm listening to and  reading so i haven't invested an awful  lot of time in the grammar because it  doesn't prevent me from understanding  uh  given that my understanding is in any  case 60 70 80 90 depending on what the  context  is  but now i'm focusing more on grammar so  again i'm helping myself notice  by investing some time in the individual  words and phrases that i'm saving by  editing the little phrase that we  capture at link by tagging it as a  genitive or dative case and then  reviewing them all  together and then i see the captured  phrase which reminds me and all of this  is helping  i read the grammar explanation again i  have a table so that when i see this  item  i'm not sure whether it is data  regenerative or what it is in some cases  and of course depending on whether it's  masculine feminine and neuter the  endings can be all over the place so i  have a table that i refer to  can't remember the table to save my life  but i go back in and i mark this as  dative  and then i'll review it and it's i'm not  really learning it but i'm making myself  more alert more attentive so that i  start to notice  better  and so you know it's a bit like  i don't know we talk about science that  we are standing on the shoulders of  giants where  new scientists are coming up with new  discoveries because they already know so  much about science that was developed  by their predecessors the newtons and  the other uh thinkers of the world well  it's the same in in language we're  noticing  more in more detail a more  uh perhaps some more sophisticated  grammatical concepts because we have the  base that we have already noticed and we  already have so the more you know the  more you can know the more you know the  more you can learn  now noticing has been identified by  linguists and grammar teachers as a very  important part of language teaching i  discovered a blog i did some research on  the web and and the the conclusions seem  to be that you know uh crash and and i  agree with this crash and all by itself  is not enough you're just reading is not  going to do it  there is more that's happening there  so some people say well you can only  sort of learn from input if you have  explicit instruction in class i don't  believe that  that's not my experience i can't take  explicit instruction in class  i want to discover it on my own  on my own terms i don't want to be in a  classroom i don't want to be in  pairs having some kind of an artificial  dialogue with someone i don't want to do  those things  that's why maybe that's just me now i'm  very interested to have reactions from  other people i am sure that the  instructional class does help you notice  things it doesn't teach it to you  now another thing that helps you notice  is to make mistakes there's no question  but you don't want to be corrected too  often and you certainly don't improve  right away and i have this experience  all the time i had it just the other day  in a discussion where a learner said  i ever read this book and i said you  can't say i ever read what do you mean  and she gave me the word in chinese and  i said aha okay i have read the book  that's all we say we don't use that  chinese term jing we don't use that term  we just say i have read the book oh  thank you very much and then she went  right back and made the same mistake  again and it's not just her i had it  with people from all different uh  language groups you can correct them and  they go right back into making the same  mistake however you've chipped away a  little bit you've chipped away a little  bit at a pattern that they have that  maybe came from their first language  you've made them a little conscious and  then you may correct them again and they  may do it in writing and then maybe  they'll see it somewhere and at some  point all of a sudden they'll notice and  and because fundamentally you can't  develop the language just by speaking  you can't develop the language just from  within it has to come from outside it  has to come from what you hear and what  you read  but the question is how can you improve  the ability of the learner to notice  what's happening because if you don't  notice it  then you'll never be able to internalize  it and use it so i think that the a big  part of input-based learning is noticing  and there are a number of things that  will help you notice and those are the  again get back to link i mean those are  the kinds of activities that we have  built into link and there's more that  we're going to bring in  uh i think reading out loud helps you  notice  different people may have different  things that help them notice but we can  do things you know over and above this  the importance of liking the language  and choosing things that are interesting  and focusing on input and doing things  that are easy and not getting stressed  out and not worrying and all those other  things we should also do things to  increase our alertness  and to help us focus our attention on  things that matter and and we can change  that focus like right now i'm on to  cases in russian i may move to verbs  later on so that was a brief discussion  on this whole issue of noticing and i  look forward to hearing your opinions  thank you
 

